#{[17592186501173 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501173 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116498" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501173 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501173 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/32/324e3b2752fd398e47ce066dfc44f8a4d96cc3d7916f917fa142485ae86bf48f.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ the width is increasing at a rate of $11\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ and the height is decreasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.$ How fast is the surface area increasing when the dimensions of the rectangular prism are $2\\,\\text{cm}$ in length, $4\\,\\text{cm}$ in width and $7\\,\\text{cm}$ in height?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501173 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6798c7d5-6dc4-50ce-934a-27d8986f8abb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501173 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $S$ be its surface area. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =4\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =7\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =11\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x,$ $y$ and $z.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2xz+2xy+2yz) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2x+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+(2x+2z)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(2z+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 4)(-5)+(2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 7)(11)+(2\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 4)(-5) \\\\  & =-60+198-110 \\\\  & =28\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the solid is increasing at a rate of $28\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501174 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501174 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343321" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501174 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501174 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ba/baa2d9cf6ae9d34de744374dbfe67e44589aa9331c1391e5fc168cd6a39bca4d.png)\n\nJennie fills an aquarium, in the shape of a rectangular prism that is $50\\,\\text{cm}$ long and $25\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, with water from a tap. How fast is the volume of water in the aquarium changing if the water level is rising at a rate of $0.2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{h}?$\n\nThe volume of water in the aquarium is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501174 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "68f6bf29-8273-5b7e-a834-752eee067f78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501174 #edb/ref 1074 "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the aquarium, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the water, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=50\\,\\text{m},\\,W=25\\,\\text{m},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=0.2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of water:\n\n$$\nV=LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWh$ with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(50)(25)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =1250\\cdot 0.2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =250.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of water in the aquarium is increasing at a rate of $250\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501185 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501185 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116080" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501185 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501185 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e1/e11ec3379c969bbc8499f36978f3a39efe1342ee6563569949dbcb61611ab5f4.png)\n\nA cube of ice is melting uniformly at a rate of $204\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min},$ where $x$ is the length of its edge. How fast is each edge of the ice block decreasing when the volume of the cube is $216\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501185 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b7d80eb-f47e-553b-8d7c-69b51e01b399" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501185 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $V$ be the volume of the ice cube. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-204\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $V=216\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\n  We start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n  \n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V=216\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 216 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=6$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-204$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -204 & =3(6)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{17}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sides of the cube are decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{17}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=6\\,\\text{cm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501196 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501196 #edb/ref 1069 "q-314534" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501196 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501196 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/81/81175de5197dac2f2f037bde6985be619c68de75a574853c61de75316ae63971.png)\n\nA rectangular swimming pool is being drained. If its length and width are $32\\text{ m}$ and $12\\text{ m}$, respectively, and the water level is falling at a rate of $0.4\\,\\text{m/min},$ how fast is the water draining out of the pool? Assume that the drain rate is positive and do not round the answer.\n\nThe water is draining out at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501196 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94a70c07-52ed-54e0-b434-f6f0663ba853" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501196 #edb/ref 1074 "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the swimming pool, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the water, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=32\\,\\text{m},\\,W=12\\,\\text{m},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-0.4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of water:\n\n$$\nV=LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWh$ with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(32)(12)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =384\\cdot (-0.4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-153.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water is draining out at a rate of $153.6\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501200 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501200 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2795" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501200 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501200 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/52/52448daf1f9aea06b4ab4713756080ddb3ab465b4fae8f92b65a54c251a81d5e.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing by $8\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}$ and its height increasing at a rate of $1\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ If the volume of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $50\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min},$  how fast is its width changing when its dimensions are $10\\,\\text{cm}$ in length, $15\\,\\text{cm}$ in width and $5\\,\\text{cm}$ in height?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501200 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a02e865a-6a6d-5b10-8171-4b544dbd46b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501200 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\text{ cm}, \\\\ y & =5\\text{ cm}, \\\\ z & =15\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-50{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=10\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=5\\,\\text{cm},$ and $z=15\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\cdot y\\cdot z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =xy(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+xz(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+yz(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-50 & =(10)(5)(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+(10)(15)(1)+(15)(5)(-8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =8\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is increasing at a rate of $8\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=10\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=5\\,\\text{cm},$ and $z=15\\,\\text{cm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501208 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501208 #edb/ref 1069 "q-314513" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501208 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501208 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7b/7b45121fc7e3b6de3e37053b944bed610e55d337b7c73d89b258a89f903ad0c2.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing by $1.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}$ and its height is increasing at a rate of $1\\text{ feet/min}.$ If the volume of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $9{\\text{ feet}}^{3}\\text{/min},$ how fast is its width decreasing when its dimensions are $5\\,\\text{feet}$ in length, $3\\,\\text{feet}$ in width, and $2\\,\\text{feet}$ in height? Assume that the rate of decrease of width is positive.\n\nThe width is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{feet}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501208 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b06ab3d9-3b8c-5c9f-a694-75f899defb02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501208 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\,\\text{feet}, \\\\ y & =3\\,\\text{feet}, \\\\ z & =2\\,\\text{feet}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5\\,\\text{feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\,\\text{feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-9\\,{\\text{feet}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=5\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=3\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=2\\,\\text{feet}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\cdot y\\cdot z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =xy(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+xz(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+yz(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9 & =(5)(3)(1)+(5)(2)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(2)(3)(-1.5) \\\\ -9 & =15+10\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})-9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is decreasing at a rate of $1.5\\,\\text{feet}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=5\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=2\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=3\\,\\text{feet}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501228 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501228 #edb/ref 1069 "q-49012" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501228 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501228 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/06/06eb8269e36ecb0185c0bfa0069c36a705e36711ca0a705fba3e6eae7b6c946d.png)\n\nJohn is a carpenter who wants to modify a wooden box. The length, width and height of the box are decreased at a rate of $1\\text{ cm/s},$ $2\\text{ cm/s}$ and $4\\text{ cm/s},$ respectively. Determine the rate at which the volume of the box decreases, if its dimensions are $15\\text{ cm}$ long, $10\\text{ cm}$ wide and $15\\text{ cm}$ high." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501228 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd9599f1-ed67-5948-967c-4c445ad154d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501228 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $L,$ $W,$ $H$ and $V$ be the length, the width, the height, and the volume of the box, respectively. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=15\\text{ cm},\\, & W=10\\text{ cm}, \\\\ H=15\\text{ cm},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-1\\text{ cm/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\text{ cm/s},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\text{ cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the four variables is given by the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ using the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(10)(15)(-1)+(15)(15)(-2)+(15)(10)(-4) \\\\  & =-1\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the box is decreasing at a rate of $1\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501232 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501232 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343194" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501232 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501232 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/99/996768e4b1ce8ae57bcf3cce61059f8349c307ea9f05c9c1f51831ff6fd8ae68.png)\n\nA rectangular loaf of rising bread dough is squeezed between two rigid side panels that keep its width fixed. The length of the loaf is decreasing by $20\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s},$ while the $60\\,\\text{mm}$ width does not change. If the volume of the loaf is constant, how fast is its height changing when its dimensions are $100\\,\\text{mm}$ in length and $30\\,\\text{mm}$ in height?\n\nThe height is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{mm}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501232 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4e15d3e-925b-56d4-bf57-253dd32acaf7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501232 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =100\\,\\text{mm}, \\\\ y & =60\\,\\text{mm}, \\\\ z & =30\\,\\text{mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-20\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =0\\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=100\\,\\text{mm},$ $y=60\\,\\text{mm}$ and $z=30\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSince $y$ is not changing and $y=60,$ we get\n\n$$\nV=60xz.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60xz) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =60x(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+60z(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =60(100)(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+60(30)(-20) \\\\ 0 & =6000(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})-36\\,000 \\\\ 36\\,000 & =6000(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =6\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the solid is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=100\\,\\text{mm},$ $y=60\\,\\text{mm}$ and $z=30\\,\\text{mm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501241 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501241 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343180" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501241 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501241 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/49/4946691a4258fb310ec7052f1edd5a1b9117ca7daeea152e4a1b01b11a624e2e.png)\n\nJennie fills an aquarium, in the shape of a rectangular prism that is $50\\,\\text{cm}$ long and $25\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, with water from a tap. What is the rate of change of the water level if the volume is increasing at a rate of $250\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{h}?$\n\nThe water level is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501241 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3d27d1e-a375-5722-8f78-27b79ebf66c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501241 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the rectangular prism, let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =50\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =25\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=250\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=50\\,\\text{cm}$ and $y=25\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\nV=(50)(25)z=1250z.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1250z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =1250(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known value into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}250 & =1250(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =0.2\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is increasing at a rate of $0.2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
